<p>A widespread blackout knocked out electricity across roughly half to two‑thirds of Cuba, including Havana, leaving millions without power on March 4. Authorities said a major failure in the national grid set off the outage. Officials said the disruption stretched from Pinar del Río in the west toward Camagüey in the center, a reminder of how fragile the system has become. News outlets tied the blackout to breakdowns in Cuba’s power system and a broader fuel crunch that limits generation. It also fits a familiar pattern: long daily outages in parts of the country, leaving households and businesses to manage with less reliable service as crews work to restore power.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/Ppcjdup4KlJrA4Hwmox8eBW_fEtv8NkcsHH7BxYh-812fSKpjeQ-584uYaqvA0YPuhjb03ZvxE3Ys2jU5Y3glwSKGw4Tyc9SMJ14p9WnNXTmGJG2NYIHxmMExTtDtXD_Q2JTMcrgfkYM2ugS843mRop58ZnQ4z5xKJAtmD5XTfGgEeCK5epAPvDFs_x4MWvljNi4MNk1CKRfyoCkJmK45vJwkymFVP4a7qD_CJYTBcgdml5h9LOwYNx9z3EA4EzL9jj5WgwreQGXfRNUqQ' alt='Malena Borrell in her shop in Havana during a blackout across half of the country on Wednesday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>What triggered it (and how unusual it was): El País traced the immediate trigger to a failure linked to the island’s main thermoelectric plant. It described the blackout as a partial collapse of the National Electric System—and the second such collapse in about a month.</li><li>How it was communicated: Cuba’s Electric Union confirmed the outage on X as it spread across large parts of the island.</li><li>How big it was (as variously described): Reports varied on the scale, with some describing about half the country affected and others putting it closer to two‑thirds.</li><li>System constraints: Several outlets pointed to tight fuel supplies and dwindling reserves, which have limited generation and fueled recurring shortages.</li><li>Policy backdrop (as described by outlets): Multiple reports linked the worsening fuel situation to U.S. actions that have restricted or blocked oil shipments—especially supplies tied to Venezuela—making it harder for Cuba to keep generators running.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Cuba’s Electric Union (UNE): The utility confirmed the outage on X and said it affected provinces from Pinar del Río to Camagüey. (<a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/04/blackout-cuba-us-oil'>The Guardian</a>)</li><li>Cuban government: Officials described the event as a partial collapse of the National Electric System, with reporting tying the disruption to a failure at a major thermoelectric plant. (<a href='https://english.elpais.com/international/2026-03-04/massive-grid-failure-leaves-twothirds-of-cuba-without-power.html'>El País</a>)</li><li>US administration (as described by outlets): Coverage framed Washington’s pressure as an “oil chokehold/blockade” that has curtailed shipments, which Cuba and some outlets link to less fuel for power generation. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/cx2ggpq1742o'>BBC News</a>)</li><li>Residents and small businesses: The Globe and Mail described the day-to-day disruption for shopkeepers in Havana trying to operate through the outage. (<a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/world/article-half-of-cuba-including-havana-without-power-in-widespread-blackout/'>The Globe and Mail</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/international/article/2026/03/04/two-thirds-of-cuba-including-havana-hit-by-blackout_6751106_4.html'>Two-thirds of Cuba, including Havana, hit by blackout</a> - lemonde.fr</li><li><a href='https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/mar/04/blackout-cuba-us-oil'>Blackout in Cuba leaves millions without power amid US oil chokehold</a> - theguardian.com</li><li><a href='https://english.elpais.com/international/2026-03-04/massive-grid-failure-leaves-twothirds-of-cuba-without-power.html'>Massive grid failure leaves two‑thirds of Cuba without power</a> - elpais.com</li><li><a href='https://www.theglobeandmail.com/